course: geography
lesson: 3
title: Projections: why Greenland looks huge
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Try pressing a piece of orange peel flat: it tears or stretches, and the same happens to the Earth's round skin on any flat map.
explain: A projection is a chosen way of flattening, and each one gives something up: true shapes, true sizes, true distances or true directions.
explain: Mercator's map of 1569 keeps compass directions right, which sailors loved, but it stretches land more and more towards the poles, so Greenland looks as big as Africa.
explain: The EU5 map is flat too, so Scandinavia, Muscovy and the far north look bigger than they really are next to Africa or India; use a globe when you want true sizes.
explain: No projection is wrong and none is perfect; the clever question is always what this map was made for.
fact: It is impossible to flatten the surface of a sphere onto paper without stretching, squashing or tearing it, so every flat map distorts something.
fact: Gerardus Mercator published his famous world map in 1569, and on it a straight line is a line of constant compass bearing.
fact: On a Mercator map Greenland looks about as large as Africa, but Africa is really about 14 times larger.
fact: At 60 degrees north a Mercator map stretches land about twice in each direction, so areas look about four times too big.
fact: A Mercator map cannot show the North or South Pole at all, because the stretching would grow without limit there.
fact: Equal-area projections such as the Gall-Peters or Mollweide maps show every country at its true size compared with others, but they bend shapes.
fact: Some maps, such as the Winkel tripel used by the National Geographic Society since 1998, accept a little error in everything to keep the whole world looking natural.
fact: Online maps such as Google Maps use a version called Web Mercator, which keeps the shapes of small areas such as streets right when you zoom in, but makes Greenland look enormous when you zoom out.
fact: The shortest route between two places on a globe is part of a great circle, a circle that goes all the way round the globe's widest part, like the equator does, so a flight from London to Los Angeles passes over Greenland and northern Canada.
fact: Russia covers about 17 million square kilometres, but Africa covers about 30 million, so Africa is almost twice as big, though Mercator makes Russia look larger.
fact: There is no single correct way to centre a world map, and many maps made in East Asia or Australia put the Pacific Ocean in the middle, while some Australian maps put south at the top.
fact: Like most game maps, the EU5 map leaves out most of the polar regions, and its flat world makes northern lands look larger than on a globe.
exercise: Why does every flat map distort something? | A sphere's surface cannot be flattened without stretching or tearing | Map makers are careless and never check their work against a globe | The Earth is really flat | Paper is too small for the world
exercise: When did Mercator publish his famous world map? | In 1569 | In 1337 | In 1884 | In 1998
exercise: What is special about a straight line on a Mercator map? | It is a line of constant compass bearing | It is always the shortest route between two places | It always follows the equator | It always runs along a border
exercise: How many times larger is Africa than Greenland? | About 14 times | About the same size | About twice as big | About 100 times
exercise: At 60 degrees north, how much too big do areas look on a Mercator map? | About four times | About twice | About ten times | They look correct
exercise: Why can a Mercator map not show the poles? | The stretching would grow without limit there | The poles are covered in ice and cannot be measured | Mercator did not know about them | The poles are too small to draw
exercise: What do equal-area maps such as the Gall-Peters get right? | The true sizes of countries | The true shapes of countries | The true compass directions | The shortest flight routes
exercise: What do equal-area maps give up? | They bend the shapes of countries | They make Africa look smaller than it is | They show no oceans | They cannot show Europe
exercise: Which projection has the National Geographic Society used since 1998? | The Winkel tripel | The Mercator | The Gall-Peters | Web Mercator
exercise: Which projection do online maps such as Google Maps use? | Web Mercator | Winkel tripel | Mollweide | Gall-Peters
exercise: What is the shortest route between two places on a globe part of? | A great circle | The equator, always | A line of latitude | A line of constant compass bearing
exercise: Which is really bigger, Russia or Africa? | Africa, almost twice as big | Russia, almost twice as big | They are exactly equal | Russia, ten times bigger
exercise: Is a map with south at the top wrong? | No, which way is up is a choice | Yes, north must always be at the top | Yes, it is against the law to print it | No, because south is the true top
exercise: What do many maps made in East Asia or Australia put in the middle? | The Pacific Ocean | The Atlantic Ocean | Europe | The North Pole
exercise: Why does Greenland look huge on many maps? | Mercator-type maps stretch land near the poles | Greenland is the largest country on Earth | Its thick white ice makes it look bigger than it is | Greenland is really as big as Africa
test: Why would a 1569 sailor care more about correct directions than correct sizes? | He steered by compass and needed a steady bearing | Sizes of countries changed every year in those days | Sailors never went near land | Directions are the same on every map ever made
test: A shape is stretched three times wider and three times taller. How many times bigger does its area look? | Nine times | Three times | Six times bigger | Twelve times
test: on google maps greenland is like as big as africa so it must be massive right? | It is big but Africa is about 14 times bigger | Yes, they are about the same size | Yes, Greenland is the biggest country | No, Greenland is smaller than Wales
test: Why does a flight from London to Los Angeles look curved on a flat map? | The great-circle route bends on a flat projection | The plane goes round storms over the Atlantic Ocean | Planes must follow lines of latitude | The pilot follows the coastline
test: Your EU5 Muscovy looks endless next to India. What should you remember? | Flat maps stretch northern lands, so check a globe | Muscovy really was bigger than all of Asia | The game map shows true sizes | India was tiny in the Middle Ages
test: Why might a compromise map like the Winkel tripel be best for a classroom wall? | It keeps every error small so the world looks natural | It shows the poles as points with no error | It has no distortion at all | It keeps all compass directions exact, like Mercator's map for sailors
test: Why do online maps care more about street shapes than country sizes? | People zoom in to find streets, where shapes must look right | Country sizes never matter to anyone | Streets are bigger than countries | Web Mercator shows the true sizes of all countries perfectly
test: Why is Antarctica often a long white strip at the bottom of maps? | The projection stretches it enormously sideways | Antarctica really is a long thin strip | It is drawn small to save space | Snow makes it look wider
test: What would happen if you tried to make a Mercator map that reached the North Pole? | It would need an endless height of paper | It would show the pole as a tiny dot at the very top | It would show the pole correctly | It would turn into a globe
test: Teacher's map puts the Pacific in the middle and Europe at the edge. A kid says "that map's broken". Who is right? | Neither map is broken, the centre is a choice | The kid, Europe must be in the middle | The kid, the Pacific is too big for the middle | The teacher, Europe-centred maps are illegal
test: Which shows country sizes correctly, a Mercator or a Mollweide map? | Mollweide, an equal-area map | Mercator, a navigation map | Both show sizes correctly | Neither can show any sizes
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principal deficiency of the Mercator projection? | It progressively exaggerates areas with increasing latitude | It misrepresents compass bearings | It exaggerates areas in the vicinity of the equator | It cannot represent the Atlantic Ocean
exam: Determine the factor by which the area of Africa exceeds that of Greenland. | Approximately fourteen | Approximately one | Approximately two | Approximately one hundred
exam: Identify the class of projection whereby the relative areas of all territories are faithfully preserved. | An equal-area projection | A Mercator projection | Web Mercator | A portolan projection of the Mediterranean
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the shortest path between two points upon the globe. | It forms part of a great circle | It invariably follows a parallel of latitude | It follows a constant compass bearing | It coincides in every case with the equator
